    },
    {
      "title": "Taxes come out first",
      "text": "Your employer withholds money from each paycheck for taxes, including federal income tax and Social Security and Medicare taxes. Withholding is an advance payment; your final tax bill may be different.",
      "imagePrompt": "A paycheck being divided into a take-home portion and a portion labeled federal tax, Social Security, and Medicare"
    },
    {
      "title": "Benefits can lower it",
      "text": "Money for health insurance or a 401(k) may also come out of your pay. Those deductions can mean less deposited now, while helping pay for coverage or save for retirement.",
      "imagePrompt": "A paycheck with separate portions directed toward health insurance and a retirement savings account, alongside the remaining deposit"
    },
    {
      "title": "A simple example",
      "text": "Suppose a paycheck starts at $2,000 before deductions. If $350 goes to tax withholding and $150 to benefits and retirement, $1,500 is left to deposit. Your actual amounts depend on your pay and choices.",
      "imagePrompt": "A sample pay stub showing $2,000 before deductions, $350 in tax withholding, $150 for benefits and retirement, and a $1,500 deposit"
    },
    {
      "title": "Check your pay stub",
      "text": "Look for gross pay, each deduction, and net pay—the amount deposited. If the gross pay or a deduction seems unexpected, ask your employer’s payroll team to explain that specific paycheck.",
      "imagePrompt": "A person reviewing a pay stub with three clearly marked sections: gross pay, deductions, and net pay"
    }
  ],
  "check": {
    "question": "Ava expected her usual paycheck, but the deposit is smaller. Her pay stub shows the expected gross pay plus new health insurance deductions. What best explains the change?",
    "options": [
      {
        "text": "The insurance deduction reduced the amount deposited.",
        "isCorrect": true,
        "feedback": "Right. Gross pay can stay the same while a new deduction lowers take-home pay."
      },
      {
        "text": "The annual salary is paid all at once.",
        "isCorrect": false,
        "feedback": "Not quite. Salary is usually paid in installments, and this pay stub shows a deduction."
      },
      {
        "text": "The tax withholding must be the final tax bill.",
        "isCorrect": false,
        "feedback": "Not quite. Withholding is an advance payment and may differ from the final tax bill."
      },
      {
        "text": "The gross pay already includes the deposit.",
        "isCorrect": false,
        "feedback": "Not quite. Gross pay is before deductions; net pay is the amount deposited."
      }
    ]
  },
  "recap": [
    "Salary is usually yearly pay before deductions; each paycheck is one installment.",
    "Taxes and benefits can be withheld before your pay is deposited.",
    "Use your pay stub to compare gross pay, deductions, and net pay."
  ],
  "goFurther": {
    "overviewCourse": "Personal Finance",
    "relatedQuestions": [
      "What does gross pay mean?",
      "How does tax withholding work?",
      "What should I look for on a pay stub?"
    ]
  }
}

{
  "title": "How a Microwave Heats Food",
  "screens": [
    {
      "title": "The Uneven Burrito",
      "text": "You heat a burrito, but one end comes out steaming while the middle is still cool. A microwave sends energy into the food, but that energy doesn’t spread evenly everywhere.",
      "imagePrompt": "A cut-open burrito with one steaming hot end and a cool center, beside a microwave"
    },
    {
      "title": "Waves Inside the Oven",
      "text": "A part inside the microwave makes invisible energy waves called microwaves. They bounce around the metal oven and pass into your food, rather than warming it only from the outside.",
      "imagePrompt": "Microwaves bouncing around the inside of an oven and entering a plate of food"
    },
    {
      "title": "Molecules Shift",
      "text": "The waves’ changing electric field makes water molecules and some charged particles in food shift back and forth. Their movement and interactions pass energy along as heat.",
      "imagePrompt": "Water molecules and charged particles in food shifting as changing electric fields pass through"
    },
    {
      "title": "Hot Spots Form",
      "text": "The waves don’t reach every part of the food with equal strength. Its shape and thickness matter, so some spots absorb more energy and heat up faster than others.",
      "imagePrompt": "A dish of food with a pattern of stronger and weaker microwave energy, creating hot and cool spots"
    },
    {
      "title": "Heat Spreads Afterward",
      "text": "Once part of the food gets hot, heat moves from warmer spots into cooler ones. Stirring soup or letting food stand gives that heat time to spread, like warmth moving through a blanket.",
      "imagePrompt": "A bowl of soup being stirred, with warmth spreading from a hot area into cooler areas"
    }
  ],
  "check": {
    "question": "After microwaving a bowl of soup, why can stirring make it more evenly warm?",
    "options": [
      {
        "text": "It mixes hot and cool spots so heat can spread.",
        "isCorrect": true,
        "feedback": "Right. Microwave energy can heat spots unevenly, and stirring mixes them together."
      },
      {
        "text": "It makes the microwave waves reach the bowl again.",
        "isCorrect": false,
        "feedback": "Not quite. Stirring doesn’t send in more waves; it mixes warmer and cooler soup."
      },
      {
        "text": "It makes all the water molecules stop moving.",
        "isCorrect": false,
        "feedback": "No. The food is warm because energy makes particles move and pass heat along."
      }
    ]
  },
  "recap": [
    "Microwaves pass into food and carry energy.",
    "That energy makes particles shift and become heat.",
    "Heating can be uneven; stirring or resting helps heat spread."
  ],
  "goFurther": {
    "overviewCourse": "Physics",
    "relatedQuestions": [
      "Why do microwaves heat some foods faster than others?",
      "Why do microwaves have turntables?",
      "Why does metal behave differently in a microwave?"
    ]
  }
}

{
  "title": "O que é inflação",
  "screens": [
    {
      "title": "A compra mudou?",
      "text": "Você vai ao supermercado com o mesmo dinheiro de sempre, mas volta com menos sacolas. Se isso acontece com muitos preços, não é só impressão: pode ser inflação.",
      "imagePrompt": "Ana no supermercado comparando uma sacola de compras cheia com uma menor, enquanto segura a mesma quantia de dinheiro."
    },
    {
      "title": "Preços em conjunto",
      "text": "Inflação é o aumento generalizado dos preços ao longo do tempo. Um produto ficar mais caro, sozinho, não basta: é preciso observar o que acontece com muitos produtos e serviços.",
      "imagePrompt": "Uma cesta com alimentos, roupas e itens de transporte, com etiquetas de preço subindo em vários itens."
    },
    {
      "title": "Seu dinheiro rende menos",
      "text": "Quando os preços sobem, a mesma quantia compra menos. Por exemplo, R$ 100 que antes pagavam uma compra maior podem não render tanto depois de um período de inflação.",
      "imagePrompt": "Uma nota de R$ 100 ao lado de duas cestas de supermercado: uma maior e outra menor, com a cesta menor representando uma compra posterior."
    },
    {
      "title": "Como se acompanha",
      "text": "No Brasil, o IPCA é um dos principais índices usados para acompanhar a inflação. Ele observa a variação dos preços de uma cesta de produtos e serviços consumidos por famílias.",
      "imagePrompt": "Uma prancheta com uma lista de compras e serviços, como comida, transporte e moradia, sendo comparada em dois momentos."
    },
    {
      "title": "Por que acontece",
      "text": "A inflação pode ter várias causas. Se muita gente quer comprar algo que está em pouca oferta, ou se custos como transporte e produção aumentam, empresas podem elevar preços. O motivo varia conforme o caso.",
      "imagePrompt": "Uma feira com muitas pessoas procurando poucos tomates, e uma entrega de mercadorias com custos de transporte indicados."
    }
  ],
  "check": {
    "question": "Em uma cafeteria, só o pão de queijo fica mais caro; os outros preços continuam iguais. O que dá para concluir?",
    "options": [
      {
        "text": "Isso, por si só, não mostra inflação generalizada.",
        "isCorrect": true,
        "feedback": "Isso mesmo. Um único preço subir não basta para indicar inflação; é preciso observar muitos produtos e serviços."
      },
      {
        "text": "Isso confirma que todos os preços estão subindo.",
        "isCorrect": false,
        "feedback": "Não. A alta de um item não mostra o que está acontecendo com os demais preços."
      },
      {
        "text": "Isso prova que o dinheiro compra mais coisas.",
        "isCorrect": false,
        "feedback": "Não. Um pão de queijo mais caro não significa que o poder de compra aumentou."
      },
      {
        "text": "Isso mostra que o IPCA mede só esse produto.",
        "isCorrect": false,
        "feedback": "Não. O IPCA acompanha uma cesta de produtos e serviços, não apenas um item."
      }
    ]
  },
  "recap": [
    "Inflação é a alta generalizada dos preços ao longo do tempo.",
    "Com preços mais altos, a mesma quantia compra menos.",
    "O IPCA acompanha preços de uma cesta de produtos e serviços no Brasil."
  ],
  "goFurther": {
    "overviewCourse": "Economia",
    "relatedQuestions": [
      "Como o IPCA é calculado?",
      "Qual é a diferença entre inflação e aumento de um preço?",
      "O que o Banco Central faz para controlar a inflação?"
    ]
  }
}

{
  "title": "Why the Sky Looks Blue",
  "screens": [
    {
      "title": "A familiar puzzle",
      "text": "Sunlight looks nearly white, but the sky above you looks blue. The difference begins as sunlight travels through the air around Earth.",
      "imagePrompt": "Show a bright white-looking Sun above a blue daytime sky, with Earth's thin layer of air visible around the planet."
    },
    {
      "title": "Sunlight has many colors",
      "text": "White sunlight is a mix of colors, like the colors in a rainbow. When air spreads light in different directions, that is called scattering.",
      "imagePrompt": "Show a beam of white sunlight entering Earth's air and separating into rainbow colors."
    },
    {
      "title": "Air spreads blue more",
      "text": "Tiny air molecules scatter short-wavelength light more strongly than long-wavelength light. Blue light gets scattered much more than red light.",
      "imagePrompt": "Show sunlight passing through air molecules, with blue rays sent in many directions and red rays continuing mostly straight."
    },
    {
      "title": "Blue reaches your eyes",
      "text": "Some of that scattered blue light reaches you from all across the sky. Violet scatters even more, but your eyes are less sensitive to it, and some is absorbed high in the air.",
      "imagePrompt": "Show a person looking upward as scattered blue light arrives from several parts of the sky, with a small amount of violet light absorbed higher in the atmosphere."
    },
    {
      "title": "Sunset takes a longer path",
      "text": "When the Sun is low, its light travels through more air to reach you. More blue gets scattered out of the direct path, so the remaining sunlight often looks orange or red.",
      "imagePrompt": "Compare sunlight traveling a short path through air at midday with a long path at sunset; show blue scattered away and orange-red light reaching the viewer."
    }
  ],
  "check": {
    "question": "At sunset, why can the Sun look orange or red?",
    "options": [
      {
        "text": "Its light travels through more air, scattering more blue away.",
        "isCorrect": true,
        "feedback": "Right. The longer path through air scatters more blue light away from the direct sunlight that reaches you."
      },
      {
        "text": "Its light travels through less air, scattering more red away.",
        "isCorrect": false,
        "feedback": "Not quite. At sunset, sunlight travels through more air, and blue—not red—is scattered more strongly."
      },
      {
        "text": "The air makes new orange light that was not in sunlight.",
        "isCorrect": false,
        "feedback": "Not quite. Sunlight already contains many colors; the air scatters some colors away more than others."
      }
    ]
  },
  "recap": [
    "Sunlight is a mix of colors, and air scatters light.",
    "Air scatters blue more strongly, sending it toward you from across the sky.",
    "At sunset, more blue is scattered out of the sunlight reaching you."
  ],
  "goFurther": {
    "overviewCourse": "Earth and Space Science",
    "relatedQuestions": [
      "Why do clouds look white?",
      "Why are sunsets often red?",
      "Why does the sky look dark from space?"
    ]
  }
}

{
