{
  "title": "How compound interest works",
  "screens": [
    {
      "title": "A growing snowball",
      "text": "Imagine rolling a snowball downhill: it picks up snow, so it gets bigger and can pick up even more. Money can grow in a similar way when it earns interest.",
      "imagePrompt": "A small snowball rolling downhill and growing larger as it gathers snow."
    },
    {
      "title": "Interest joins your balance",
      "text": "Interest is money paid for letting someone use your money, or charged for borrowing money. When earned interest is added to your savings, your balance gets bigger.",
      "imagePrompt": "A savings jar with a small pile of coins labeled as interest being added to the coins already inside."
    },
    {
      "title": "Growth earns more growth",
      "text": "After interest is added, future interest can be based on the larger balance. Earning interest on your original money and on past interest is called compound interest.",
      "imagePrompt": "A simple three-step sequence showing a balance growing as interest is added, then the larger balance earning more interest."
    },
    {
      "title": "A simple example",
      "text": "Suppose $100 earns 10% interest in a year. It gains $10, making $110. The next year, 10% of $110 is $11—not just $10—because the first year's interest stayed in the account.",
      "imagePrompt": "A two-year timeline: $100 grows to $110 after gaining $10, then to $121 after gaining $11."
    },
    {
      "title": "Savings and borrowing",
      "text": "Compounding can help savings grow, but it can also make debt grow when interest is added to what you owe. Actual results depend on the rate, timing, fees, and taxes; ask a financial professional about a specific account or loan.",
      "imagePrompt": null
    }
  ],
  "check": {
    "question": "A savings account starts with $200. It earns interest, and that interest stays in the account. Why could it earn more interest in the following year?",
    "options": [
      {
